WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.982
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:20.850
INTERVIEWER: So this is the
150th anniversary celebration

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.120
interview with Professor
Isadore Singer.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.910
And let me start by asking,
where you were born?

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.020
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.220
SINGER: I was born
in Detroit, Michigan.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.299
I'll start over again.

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:33.049
INTERVIEWER: Okay.

00:00:33.049 --> 00:00:34.540
SINGER: Where was I born?

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:40.200
I was born in Detroit, Michigan,
in 1924, and I grew up there.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.570
My parents came from Poland and
emigrated to Toronto, Canada,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.030
where they met and married.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:53.190
And then they came
to Detroit in 1923.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.390
INTERVIEWER: And what
did your parents do?

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.480
Did they also work?

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.920
SINGER: My father was a printer
and my mother a seamstress.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.590
When they arrived, my
father tells a story

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:08.620
that the printers'
union was on strike.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.470
And they asked him not
to break the strike

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.790
and gave him $5 a day not
to do that, and he agreed.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.380
He wrote his mother
back in Poland

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.300
that it is, in fact, true
that the streets of the United

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.040
States were paved with gold.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:31.690
We were very poor
during the Depression.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.790
It's a vivid memory
for me, and so I'm

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:38.090
particularly concerned about
people who are out of jobs

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.790
now, because my father was
out of a job for a year or so.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.150
And we suffered
badly at that time.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:51.370
INTERVIEWER: Were there any
particular events or influences

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:56.890
in your childhood that you think
helped you to choose the career

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:57.655
path you chose?

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:02.980
SINGER: No.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:07.720
You know, I was an all-A
student all the time.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.110
Mostly, I found school boring.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:15.100
I preferred playing baseball
and stuff like that.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.690
I was more interested
in athletics

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.410
than I was in anything
going on at school.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.210
I read a great deal.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:28.490
Things did come to an end about
my junior year in high school.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.170
I had a very good
English teacher,

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.280
and I had a very good
chemistry teacher.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.740
So a periodic table struck me
as something very exciting,

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:43.500
and the English teacher got me
to read lots of good books--

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.620
Dostoyevsky, War and Peace.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.900
My oldest daughter's
named Natasha

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.780
after Natasha in War and Peace.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.780
So those two things
had a big impact on me

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.830
and woke me up to the
excitement of science.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.300
I became president of the
Science Club very quickly

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.550
and gave lectures on
relativity already

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.680
as a senior in high school.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:16.100
INTERVIEWER: How did you decide
on the University of Michigan?

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:21.410
SINGER: They had a
competition, and I either

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.660
came first or second
in this competition,

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.360
and therefore, I
won a scholarship

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.760
to the University of Michigan.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.870
What that meant was
I had tuition paid.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:36.570
Tuition was $50 a semester.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:43.070
As I say, we were very
poor, and I was originally

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.670
going to go to Wayne
University and live at home.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:48.950
But my mother said, you can't.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.030
You can't give up
$50 twice a year.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.850
So I went to
Michigan as a result.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:58.440
I lived in a co-op.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:04.880
In the co-op I lived
in, you paid $2.25

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.240
a week for room and board.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.890
And I remember the vivid
discussion about getting up

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:18.250
to $2.50 a week, which we did.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.000
INTERVIEWER: It must
be staggering to look

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.220
at tuition prices now.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.950
SINGER: Absolutely.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.610
It's shocking, actually.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.310
But of course, there are a
lot of amenities that one

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.900
doesn't take into account.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.822
We didn't have any at all.

00:04:31.822 --> 00:04:36.630
In fact, I survived by having
three jobs at any one time

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.460
and also by selling
my football tickets,

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:46.310
which I could sell
illegally for $5 a ticket.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:50.100
That lasted two weeks for me.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:55.250
At any rate, the war started
when I was a freshman.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:59.270
And I enlisted in
the Signal Corps,

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.110
because they said they would
allow me to continue school

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:06.260
until I finished, as
long as I took courses

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:10.130
in electrical
engineering, which I did.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.430
Because they wanted me to
become a radar officer when

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.140
I finally got in the army.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.350
I might add that I rushed
through school because I didn't

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:27.350
really believe that they would
let me go the full four years,

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.710
and I got my degree
in 2 and 1/2 years.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:34.430
INTERVIEWER: That must
have been difficult.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:38.317
SINGER: Well, I've gotten
used to being at MIT,

00:05:38.317 --> 00:05:40.400
with everything going on
and so many things to do.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:47.450
INTERVIEWER: So what is it
that drew you to physics,

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.120
do you think?

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:54.380
SINGER: Because of my lectures
on relativity in high school,

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.900
I felt that I didn't
really understand

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.840
the question what the issue was,
even though I spoke about it.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.550
And so I wanted to learn more
about relativity, and I I'd

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.890
heard about quantum mechanics.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:11.050
And so I wanted to
learn that, as well.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:15.290
Actually, when I
got to Michigan,

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.020
I hadn't decided whether
to major in physics

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.680
or in English literature.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.650
And what happened
was that I found

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.790
students in the poetry
class understood poetry,

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.070
many of them,
instinctively, easily,

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:32.840
and I struggled with it.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.120
But when I took
physics, I was the one

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.610
who understood what was
going on very quickly,

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:42.013
and that decided me as to
what path I should take.

00:06:42.013 --> 00:06:44.180
INTERVIEWER: That's a good
way to make the decision.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:47.130
SINGER: That's what I think
the first couple of years

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.930
at a university is all about.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.400
INTERVIEWER: And then how
did the interests shift

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.700
from physics to math?

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.730
SINGER: That's kind
of complicated.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.340
As I say, I finished
early, went into the army.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.860
Was in the army for
2 and 1/2 years.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.530
But after a year and a half,
when I was destined for

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:20.910
was greater officer in
the European sector.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:24.720
And that was about
ended, and we were all

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.260
moved to go to the Pacific.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:31.800
I was still concerned
that, as an undergraduate,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.750
I didn't understand relativity
or quantum mechanics.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:41.820
And I had heard about
extension courses

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.960
at the University of Chicago.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.740
So on my way, everybody--
it was called redeployment

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.980
at that time, all of us
moving from the East Coast

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:50.560
to the West.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.080
I stopped in Chicago and
went up to the fourth floor

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.590
of the building.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.920
And there was-- I've
forgotten his name.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:07:59.940
[INAUDIBLE], I think.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.540
At any rate, he told me he
did have extension courses

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:06.630
in geometry and then
group theory, which was

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.950
needed for quantum mechanics.

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:14.260
And so I enrolled in them,
and I took them with me.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:18.120
I ended up in the
Philippines and ended up

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:23.520
running a communications
school for the Filipino army

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.050
when I was out there.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:29.580
And I would get
these letters from--

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.130
corrected papers of mine.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:33.299
I studied all the time.

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:38.590
My friends played poker,
and I did my homework.

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:40.140
But to answer your
question, how did

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:43.049
I get into math,
what happened was

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:46.290
since all the textbooks for
these correspondence courses

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:49.350
were from the
University of Chicago,

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.690
I thought that would
be the place to go.

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:56.730
And I was, indeed, accepted
in the math department,

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.850
because I thought I wanted
more math to understand

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:03.760
the physics of quantum mechanics
and relativity, which was,

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:06.060
in fact, the right instinct.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:11.490
But that first year in math was
so wonderful and so beautiful,

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:14.960
whereas listening in on the
quantum mechanics course,

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:16.935
it was still the same--

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:27.000
a rough course, I would say.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:32.170
Then I felt mathematics
was really my field.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.870
And I was right, because it was
a mathematical instinct that

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:38.280
told me the way they were
teaching quantum mechanics

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:41.130
was not right.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:44.028
That is, in fact, true
as far as I'm concerned.

00:09:44.028 --> 00:09:46.570
INTERVIEWER: You decided to stay
at the University of Chicago

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:47.890
for your doctorate.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:49.510
SINGER: Yes.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:51.302
INTERVIEWER: Why?

00:09:51.302 --> 00:09:53.260
SINGER: Well, that first
year was so wonderful.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:59.500
And remember, I didn't have
a great background in math.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:03.470
But a lot of friends
supported me and taught me

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:05.230
the material I didn't know.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:07.390
I rapidly caught up.

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:11.660
And the faculty was
just terrific there.

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:13.060
Brought new people in--

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:16.830
Andre Weil, one of the
world's great mathematicians.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:22.220
Chern, SS Chern, came the last
year, who influenced me greatly

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:23.980
in differential geometry.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:27.950
And we became very good friends
over a long period of time.

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:30.050
So that was the place to be.

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:32.680
INTERVIEWER: So would you
consider him a mentor?

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.820
SINGER: My two mentors
were Chern, certainly,

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.960
and Irving Segal, who
was my thesis advisor

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.060
and eventually came to MIT.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:50.032
I learned a great deal
from both of them.

00:10:50.032 --> 00:10:52.240
INTERVIEWER: I wonder if
you'd talk a little bit more

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:55.130
about Irving Segal?

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:56.520
SINGER: About Irving Segal?

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.400
INTERVIEWER: Mm-hmm.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:04.960
SINGER: Well, he was a
wonderful and lousy teacher

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.120
at the same time.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:08.680
We were excited
about what he was

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:14.680
doing, because he was lecturing
on the forefront of analysis

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.260
and functional analysis.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.960
But he got stuck about
halfway through every lecture,

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.780
and he spent the rest
of the lecture trying

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:23.990
to straighten it out.

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:26.620
So that makes him
a lousy teacher.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.840
He didn't get very
far in any one day.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.900
But I'd go home and
would try and solve

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.470
the problem he was stuck with.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:37.590
And I'd either have my solution
or I didn't know how to do it,

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:39.040
so I was very eager
to see what he

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:43.330
was going to do at the
next meeting in the class.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:46.840
And I could compare my solution
with his, if I had one,

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:51.190
or I could see where
he did something

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:52.510
that I hadn't understood.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:56.260
And so it was essentially
doing research every time

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:57.830
on your own.

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:01.450
So that's one way in which he
was a very exciting teacher.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:03.250
Also, he stayed late at night.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.920
And most of us, as students,
stayed late at night.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.300
His door was always open.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.530
So late at night, I
could go in, and it

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:14.940
wasn't a matter of talking
about my dissertation.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.020
If we did, that was
for about five minutes.

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:19.120
But we'd spend the rest
of the time talking

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.070
about mathematics
and the importance

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:23.690
of functional analysis.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:26.740
So that's how he
influenced me a great deal

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:30.280
and gave me a very good
background in analysis.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:32.890
Chern came the last
year I was there.

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:38.370
And his field is
differential geometry.

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:41.380
And I only listened to
his course and took notes,

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:44.030
but I immediately felt that
I was in the wrong field,

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:47.620
I was so taken by
his geometric view

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:51.730
and the instruments
he used in order

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:55.000
to reflect that geometric view.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.670
And it was too late for
me to make any changes,

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:02.530
but you'll see that it had a
great impact on me and on MIT

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:04.760
when we get to it.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:06.940
INTERVIEWER: So how
did you come to MIT?

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:11.420
SINGER: Well, when I got my
degree, I had two offers.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:18.064
One to the Institute for
Advanced Study to be with--

00:13:18.064 --> 00:13:19.251
what's the matter with me?

00:13:22.939 --> 00:13:25.170
I've forgotten.

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:27.940
And MIT, I had an offer.

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:32.320
And that offer was
$50 more than the one

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:35.200
from the Institute
for Advanced Study.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.330
So, naturally, I took it.

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:43.240
I had a family already, and
that was the thing to do.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:48.220
INTERVIEWER: Do you remember
what your first impressions

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:50.470
were of MIT?

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:53.270
SINGER: I'm so glad you asked
me about my first impression.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:55.600
So you said you liked
stories, and I'm

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:01.280
going to tell you one
I've often spoke about.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:07.900
In fact, I'm going to reminisce,
because I'm retiring June 30th.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:12.040
And I arrived at
MIT July 1, 1950.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:16.420
So that's 60 years,
with some time away.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:21.640
But July 1, we'll
celebrate the 60 years,

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:24.640
and I'm about to tell
you the first day.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.590
So I came over across--

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:28.430
I was staying in Back Bay--

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:31.400
across the river and
went to Building 2

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:34.730
to meet Ted Martin,
who was the chairman.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:37.810
And he had one
secretary, Ruth Goodwin.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:38.920
Went into the office.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:42.640
There was a guy
reading The Globe.

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:45.910
And I told Ruth who I was.

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:49.210
He put down his paper
and said, I'm Ambrose.

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:51.790
Singer, we've got a seminar
going on Lie groups.

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:53.560
Come on, let's go.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.737
I said, but I have to
see Professor Martin.

00:14:56.737 --> 00:14:57.820
He said, oh, that'll keep.

00:14:57.820 --> 00:14:58.940
Come on.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:00.790
So I went to the
seminar, and I met

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:04.270
a number of people who
got to be good friends,

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:07.240
some mathematicians of note.

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:10.600
George White,
particularly, in topology.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:15.520
And then we went back and
I did talk to the chair,

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.010
and Ambrose waited for me.

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:23.710
And then he said, we meet
for coffee at 11:00 PM.

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:24.430
I'll pick you up.

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:26.560
Where do you stay?

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:30.490
So he did pick me up
that night around 11:00,

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:33.160
and we went to this
terrible coffee shop--

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:37.270
Hayes Bickford in the
middle of nowhere in Boston.

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:41.680
And there, I met the same
group who were in the seminar.

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:43.990
And Kate Whitehead,
George Whitehead's wife,

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.750
was there, and also
a mathematician.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:48.830
And we talked for a bit.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:52.360
And then I thought Ambrose
was going to take me home.

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:53.440
He did not.

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.280
He simply showed me
what Boston was about,

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:00.730
drove around for a long time,
and dropped me off about 3:00

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:02.680
in the morning.

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:09.000
And I went up to
this apartment that I

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:13.250
had been given for a few days.

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:16.380
You have to remember
that I hadn't been home,

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:19.110
because I'd left
for the university.

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:21.720
I then spent three
years in the army,

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:26.340
and I then spent 3 and 1/2 years
at the University of Chicago,

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:28.140
so I really had no home.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:31.950
I went up to my bedroom
and said, I'm home.

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:34.470
This is my home.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:37.670
And that's my first day at MIT.

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:39.120
INTERVIEWER: Do you remember--

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:48.060
so you had had experience
at two other universities.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.640
Was there anything that
struck you as different

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:53.280
when you got on the
campus and kind of got

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.920
to know the students and the
faculty and the culture here?

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:02.910
SINGER: Not immediately,
but as time went on,

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:06.440
I saw a considerable difference
between the two places.

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:08.510
One of the important
things at Chicago

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:10.550
was it was a graduate school.

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:12.520
For us, undergraduate
teaching was

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:17.410
done by another division
of the university.

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:21.800
As a graduate student, we saw
very little of undergraduates,

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:23.060
brilliant as they were.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.520
I would mostly see
them in the locker room

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:29.090
or in some sport
or another and had

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:30.690
very little contact with them.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:32.300
I did no teaching there.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:35.130
MIT was entirely different.

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:43.130
It was an important and
enormous load or responsibility

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.350
of teaching undergraduates.

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:48.440
And that has been done
with great aplomb,

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.550
as far as I'm concerned.

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:54.520
So MIT was a place
where you did research,

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.824
but teaching was
extremely important.

00:18:02.863 --> 00:18:04.530
INTERVIEWER: Was there
anything about it

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:07.290
that was a surprise
when you've sort of got

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:08.820
to know undergraduates?

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:14.280
SINGER: Was there
a surprise at MIT?

00:18:18.538 --> 00:18:20.080
INTERVIEWER: Either
in their attitude

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:25.030
or what you found you
got from the relationship

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:26.350
or anything like that?

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:33.730
SINGER: Well, it took a while
for me to think in those terms,

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:34.240
I must say.

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:41.460
What happened was that
Ambrose asked me what

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:44.920
Chern had done in his course.

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:52.630
And he wanted me to discuss it
and explain it to everybody.

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:55.930
He was a stickler for details.

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:59.650
So I ended up more or less
lecturing on differential

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:06.400
geometry to him,
learning it with him,

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.240
and reorganizing it into
a more modern language

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:11.950
than Chern used.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:13.390
We gave courses in it.

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:17.870
Students wrote books about it.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:21.730
And so MIT became a center
for a differential geometry

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:27.790
on the basis of what we
tried to do with, really,

00:19:27.790 --> 00:19:30.850
the notes of my
course from Chicago.

00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:34.180
Now, as to the difference,
the feeling that I had

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:36.430
was that I could--

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:39.220
there was a kind of freedom.

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:43.630
I could give a course
immediately on new material

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:45.730
to undergraduates.

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:51.760
And recently, in fact, because I
was looking for some old notes,

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:55.990
I was really shocked at how much
large undergraduate teaching I

00:19:55.990 --> 00:19:59.860
did at the time with the freedom
of reorganizing the course.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:03.190
I gave a course in-- it was
called Algebra Matrix Theory,

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:07.330
and I just changed it
completely into a modern form.

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:10.750
And I still have former
students who are now well-known

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:13.720
mathematicians who remind
me, whenever we meet,

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:18.760
that I had given this remarkable
course with a huge audience--

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:23.740
2190, the big room.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:26.020
I gave lectures in
there frequently,

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:27.890
and I was allowed to do that--

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:31.160
criticized, of course,
by some old timers.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:35.630
But there was a freedom
to change courses.

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:39.820
So as to adapt to modern times.

00:20:39.820 --> 00:20:43.000
I must tell you
that the '50s was

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:46.690
a period of great
development in mathematics.

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:51.130
And so between seminars,
reorganizing courses,

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:53.760
and keeping up with
what was happening--

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:56.350
topology, geometry.

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:58.720
A lot of things were
happening at that time.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:01.451
We had many seminars.

00:21:01.451 --> 00:21:04.720
We could immediately put
them in the coursework,

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:08.440
and we were allowed a freedom
to do that that I had not known

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:11.930
before, but only
because I wasn't--

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:13.780
you know, I couldn't
do that at Chicago,

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:14.890
because I was a student.

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:17.200
Whereas, here, I wasn't
a student anymore.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:19.570
And that was really--

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:20.982
those were exciting times.

00:21:20.982 --> 00:21:22.690
INTERVIEWER: Is there
a particular reason

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:26.370
the '50s was such a busy
time for mathematics?

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:27.870
SINGER: Yeah, because
the war ended.

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:32.505
INTERVIEWER: Just
the influx of people?

00:21:32.505 --> 00:21:33.880
SINGER: Well,
there were a number

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:36.430
of very good French
mathematicians

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:41.530
who were prisoners of war
or conscientious objectors,

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:45.100
and they were one of the
leaders in this development.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:50.800
So we got a lot of new
stuff coming out of France.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:53.920
And then there was Chern
and what he was doing

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:56.590
and what he had done and
the implications of it.

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:03.460
So a lot of things were
happening that were brand new.

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:05.940
The '50s were very special.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:11.420
INTERVIEWER: So
can we sort of walk

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:16.550
through the various
areas of interest

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:18.470
that you've had over
the years, starting

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:21.800
with differential geometry?

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:25.050
Will you talk about the
work you've done in that

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:28.790
and maybe why it's particularly
interesting to you,

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:30.969
why it's captured
your attention?

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:39.390
SINGER: I think
what I really like

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:41.340
in mathematics is
the connections

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:43.680
between different fields.

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:48.840
And geometry
captured my attention

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:55.980
because it combined topology
with geometric insight.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:23:01.410
And you needed analysis, which
I learned from Irving Segal,

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:02.280
in order to--

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:08.030
well, what we call in order
to get existence theorems.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:11.700
In order to solve certain
equations which came up

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:16.820
from geometry, you needed
some strength in analysis

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:19.020
in order to solve
those equations.

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:22.170
And I like that combination
of all three things,

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:25.660
and that's what
attracted me to geometry.

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:28.530
And in fact, again,
what we did at MIT

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:33.840
was all the courses that
needed these backgrounds

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:40.760
in other fields,
Ambrose and I revised.

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:44.400
And we revised the geometry
course, as well, to be modern.

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:46.960
And I think, among them
all, the geometry course

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:51.360
is pretty much what it
was when we revised them--

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:53.700
by then, the '60s.

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:57.660
INTERVIEWER: I assume they had
not been revised for a while

00:23:57.660 --> 00:23:58.705
before that.

00:23:58.705 --> 00:23:59.580
SINGER: That's right.

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.440
They were pretty old-fashioned.

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:08.790
This was an opportunity of
Young Turks coming in and doing

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:10.752
some new things.

00:24:10.752 --> 00:24:12.210
INTERVIEWER: It
was your impression

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:17.082
that the mathematics department
was a little behind the times

00:24:17.082 --> 00:24:17.790
when you arrived?

00:24:21.565 --> 00:24:22.440
SINGER: I don't know.

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:27.480
The only department I had been
acquainted with was Chicago,

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:29.340
and it certainly was
not behind the times.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:37.580
I guess maybe MIT was a
little and we were the first--

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:40.220
Ambrose was the
leader, I must say.

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:42.690
It was his energy that said
we should do this, that,

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:43.920
and the other thing.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:50.280
And I provided some of the
energy to help him along.

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:55.650
And so it did need
some modernization

00:24:55.650 --> 00:25:01.260
in the '50s, though we had
terrific people like George

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:02.430
Whitehead.

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:06.030
And Norbert Wiener
was still there,

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:08.680
but he was doing his own thing.

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:14.160
And his main contributions
to mathematics

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.725
were already finished by then.

00:25:16.725 --> 00:25:18.975
INTERVIEWER: Well, that's
probably why they hired you.

00:25:18.975 --> 00:25:19.680
SINGER: Pardon?

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:23.520
INTERVIEWER: That's
probably why they hired--

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:30.810
bring someone the University
of Chicago newer stuff to MIT.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:32.730
SINGER: Well, it took
a while, you know?

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:35.375
I always kid the department.

00:25:35.375 --> 00:25:38.820
They had a program of
instructors, Moore instructors,

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:42.930
and I was a Moore instructor
there from '50 to '52.

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:45.570
But they did not keep me on.

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:48.270
It was only supposed to
be a two-year appointment,

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:49.350
and most of us left.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.600
And I went to UCLA.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:55.895
But I say they sent me to the
minor leagues for training,

00:25:55.895 --> 00:25:57.390
and they did.

00:25:57.390 --> 00:25:59.570
And it was good
for me, actually,

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:01.110
and I came back
four years later.

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:06.040
And that's really when I
started revising the department.

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:09.480
And I think Ambrose and
Whitehead, who were here,

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:11.460
were very eager to
have me come back,

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:17.550
and that's why the department
decided to have me come back.

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:20.640
INTERVIEWER: What about the
area of partial differential

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:22.050
equations?

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:24.420
SINGER: That's part of
what I call analysis,

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:27.570
and those equations are
what come up in differential

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:29.760
geometry many times.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:31.830
And it's solving
those equations that

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:36.120
are critical for many
problems in geometry.

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:41.080
INTERVIEWER: And I have
mathematical physics?

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:42.370
SINGER: Yes, that came later.

00:26:46.330 --> 00:26:49.400
Mathematical
physics-- my interest

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:52.940
in mathematical physics
started with something

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:58.510
called self-dual solutions, to
partial differential equations.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:02.650
And those self-dual
equations were much simpler

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:06.550
than the usual ones you find
in what's called gauge theory.

00:27:06.550 --> 00:27:11.830
And I was struck by the
physicists Polyakov and company

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:17.410
coming up with these geometric
solutions to equations.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:21.040
And we mathematicians
had not seen them.

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:27.340
And I felt that it was
a blind spot on our part

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:30.370
not to have understood
that these equations could

00:27:30.370 --> 00:27:32.440
be simplified this way.

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:34.210
And then I got interested
in saying, well,

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:35.740
why do they want these?

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:37.060
What's happening?

00:27:37.060 --> 00:27:40.600
And this has to do with what's
called gauge theory in physics,

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:43.540
which turns out to be
something of mathematics--

00:27:43.540 --> 00:27:46.340
fiber bundles that
we knew in geometry.

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:49.210
So I was struck by what
they had done, struck

00:27:49.210 --> 00:27:53.380
about this dictionary called
the Wu-Yang Dictionary, that

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:56.530
related mathematical terms
in geometry to physics

00:27:56.530 --> 00:27:59.120
terms in gauge theory.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:02.580
And so I wanted to know where
all this was leading to,

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:08.640
and I started to
seminar and a course

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:11.490
on the connections
between math and physics.

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:13.690
Now, I did not do that here.

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:20.140
In '77, I decided,
for personal reasons,

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:23.180
to go to the West Coast,
and I went to Berkeley.

00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:25.980
And I spent seven
years at Berkeley.

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:30.100
MIT was not happy about
that, and what they did

00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:36.580
is ask me to retire and make
sure that I could return easily

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:39.010
and come out of retirement.

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:43.930
So I started the
seminar in Berkeley.

00:28:43.930 --> 00:28:45.730
And every year, I
got a call from MIT

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:48.160
to ask me to come back.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:54.010
And I got very
impatient with Berkeley.

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:57.610
And when they made the critical
call, I said, yes, I'm coming.

00:28:57.610 --> 00:28:58.930
I'm coming home.

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:00.130
And I did.

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:04.450
INTERVIEWER: What was
different about the atmosphere

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:08.650
or culture at Berkeley
that didn't fit

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:10.580
as well as the MIT one?

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:13.820
SINGER: As I said, I went
there for personal reasons,

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:16.270
not for scientific reasons.

00:29:16.270 --> 00:29:18.925
But there, I did witness
a change in culture.

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:25.660
It's as if the
younger people felt

00:29:25.660 --> 00:29:28.360
that there were some connections
between math and physics

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:32.860
that hadn't been
explored, and they

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:37.720
were excited about my running
a course and giving a seminar.

00:29:37.720 --> 00:29:39.250
And three of my
best students are

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:41.560
students that I
had at Berkeley who

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:46.840
got involved in this
connection between geometry

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:50.580
and mathematics and
case theory and physics,

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:53.260
wrote her thesis
about these matters.

00:29:53.260 --> 00:29:56.460
And all three of them are
very close friends who I know.

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:07.100
INTERVIEWER: So why did you
ultimately decide to come back?

00:30:07.100 --> 00:30:13.500
SINGER: Berkeley, politically,
I didn't care for mixture.

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:17.400
And the University, although
it had some brilliant people,

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:23.510
by and large, there were people
who were still involved in--

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:30.420
I don't know-- revolutionary
times, political issues.

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:33.330
And they had more to
say at the University

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:36.030
than the really brilliant
people who were there.

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:41.460
And ultimately, I got impatient
with all that politics

00:30:41.460 --> 00:30:44.580
interfering with real science.

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:47.553
And it was one of
those days where

00:30:47.553 --> 00:30:52.170
I was fuming that MIT
called me again and asked

00:30:52.170 --> 00:30:53.970
me to come back, and I did.

00:30:56.620 --> 00:31:01.160
INTERVIEWER: Is this a good
time to talk about your theorem

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:03.780
and how that came about?

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:05.410
SINGER: Yes.

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:08.070
But I'd like to have one more--

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:11.310
we're talking about stories.

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:16.230
My wife and two daughters were
very unhappy about moving east.

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:21.570
And they always said that they
wanted to go back, especially

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.870
my children, my two daughters.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:28.110
Well, it now happens
that daughter Emily

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:32.190
is biotech editor of
technology reviews here,

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:36.780
and my other daughter,
Annabel, received her PhD

00:31:36.780 --> 00:31:41.040
in brain and cognitive sciences
at the University of California

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:45.720
San Francisco and elected
to be a postdoc here at MIT.

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:51.760
So now they're both here,
and I see them frequently.

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:54.670
INTERVIEWER: So you
knew best after all.

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:58.720
SINGER: I wouldn't know,
but it's worked out.

00:31:58.720 --> 00:32:03.670
INTERVIEWER: So how did your
collaboration and your theorem

00:32:03.670 --> 00:32:04.390
develop?

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:05.750
Can you tell me that story?

00:32:05.750 --> 00:32:06.250
SINGER: Yes.

00:32:10.540 --> 00:32:12.250
I got to know
Michael Atiyah when

00:32:12.250 --> 00:32:16.310
I was at the Institute for
Advanced Study in 1955.

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:17.430
That was a very good year.

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:21.070
We had a lot of top
mathematicians there,

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:23.630
and I got to know Michael well.

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:29.230
And there was a change in
my family circumstances,

00:32:29.230 --> 00:32:32.390
and I was on sabbatical.

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:37.610
And I needed to go to someplace
where English was spoken.

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:39.620
And so I called Michael.

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:41.930
He himself had just
arrived at Oxford,

00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:45.750
and I asked whether
there was a place for me.

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:47.970
And he simply said, come.

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:49.510
We'll arrange it.

00:32:49.510 --> 00:32:56.290
So my wife and I and
son moved to Oxford.

00:32:56.290 --> 00:33:00.640
And after I got settled
in the first day,

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:06.120
I was warming myself
by one of those fires.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:09.370
And he came up and he
asked me a question, which

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:10.710
was a question in his field.

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:16.720
Why was the A-roof genus an
integer for spin manifolds?

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:18.510
That was the question.

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:20.410
And I looked at him
and I said, Michael,

00:33:20.410 --> 00:33:22.930
why are you asking
me that question?

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:25.330
You know the answer to that.

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:27.700
Really probing him that
he had some other reason

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:29.110
for asking it.

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:31.690
He said, well, yes,
I do know the answer.

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:33.640
But I feel there's a
deeper reason for it.

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:37.840
I liked that answer.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.520
And although I was working
on another problem,

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:45.020
I spent the spring in the
gardens of Oxford colleges

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:51.260
sitting on benches thinking
about that problem.

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:54.570
And finally, I thought
I knew the answer to it.

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:59.990
And it involved the Dirac
operator, Dirac's equation

00:33:59.990 --> 00:34:04.730
for a spinning electron, which
I knew for other reasons,

00:34:04.730 --> 00:34:07.520
and involved
differential equations

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:10.639
and analysis and some geometry.

00:34:10.639 --> 00:34:15.199
So the next day, I came to
Michael's office and said,

00:34:15.199 --> 00:34:17.960
look, Michael, I think
you're getting an integer

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:21.469
because it's the number of
solutions to this equation.

00:34:21.469 --> 00:34:25.070
Moreover, if we do a
little bit of juggling,

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:28.380
you'll see that all the
equations that give you

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:31.310
integers as answers can
all be described in terms

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:34.969
of these geometric operators.

00:34:34.969 --> 00:34:38.060
You're just counting
the number of solutions,

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:41.030
and that's why you're
getting integers.

00:34:41.030 --> 00:34:43.790
It just took
overnight for Michael

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:46.790
to say, yeah, we can write
down a formula for all that.

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:48.560
And that started
our collaboration

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:51.020
on the index theorem.

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:54.800
And actually, nine
months from the time

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:57.740
he asked me the
question, at that time

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:02.640
we had a complete proof
of the index theorem.

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:04.490
INTERVIEWER: You had a new baby.

00:35:04.490 --> 00:35:05.410
SINGER: What a baby.

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:08.990
It grew into really something--

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:11.390
very robust development.

00:35:11.390 --> 00:35:14.540
INTERVIEWER: So as someone
you know without a science

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:19.190
background, is there a way
you can explain the theorem

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:20.480
in kind of lay terms?

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:27.472
And I understand if
that's not possible.

00:35:27.472 --> 00:35:28.930
SINGER: Well, it's
difficult to do.

00:35:36.470 --> 00:35:40.320
I can just give you motivation
for it, to some extent.

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:43.163
You're trying to
solve an equation,

00:35:43.163 --> 00:35:44.580
and you want to
find the solution.

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:51.030
Something called the
phantom alternative,

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:57.450
where you look at the number
of solutions and you also look

00:35:57.450 --> 00:36:03.720
at the number of different
ways you cannot get a solution.

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:05.700
And you compare the
difference of the two.

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:12.170
What you'd really like is
to have that equal to zero,

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:18.300
and that difference is called
the index of this equation.

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:20.310
You'd like it to be the
zero, because then it

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:26.190
would say that existence
implies uniqueness,

00:36:26.190 --> 00:36:28.960
and uniqueness
implies existence.

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:35.400
Existence is-- well, any rate,
those are the two integers.

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:38.220
If they're equal, then
you know, as I said,

00:36:38.220 --> 00:36:39.870
existence implies uniqueness.

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:42.420
Uniqueness implies existence.

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:49.740
Well, it's much easier
to show uniqueness

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:52.300
than there is existence.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:55.530
So if you know
it's zero, you only

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:57.450
have to solve half the problem.

00:36:57.450 --> 00:37:01.600
And you only solve the
easier half of the problem.

00:37:01.600 --> 00:37:03.890
And that was the
original motivation

00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:07.350
for defining or saying what
you meant by the index.

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:11.010
And the reason our
theorem was so exciting,

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:12.600
aside from the fact
that it combined

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:15.120
a lot of different
fields, is it gave you

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:18.360
an answer in geometric terms--

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:22.320
the number of solutions,
or the number of holes

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:25.770
in this particular, you see.

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:31.480
So it put geometry into this
partial differential equation

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:33.490
answer.

00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:36.270
And that's what
made it exciting.

00:37:36.270 --> 00:37:40.410
INTERVIEWER: Can
you explain how that

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:45.660
led to this sort of
breakdown between fields,

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:49.500
between physics and
math, for example?

00:37:49.500 --> 00:37:51.690
SINGER: Well, there are
two questions, I think,

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:53.820
when you say the
breakdown between.

00:37:53.820 --> 00:37:56.510
One is in mathematics itself.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:38:01.335
This had such impact because we
were using geometry, topology,

00:38:01.335 --> 00:38:05.520
a little algebraic geometry,
and analysis all together

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:07.800
to get a very striking solution.

00:38:07.800 --> 00:38:12.870
So that broke down barriers
between fields a great deal.

00:38:12.870 --> 00:38:16.890
And because it was so
striking, those barriers

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:25.770
have stayed down all this time,
except for people who were very

00:38:25.770 --> 00:38:28.860
fixed in a given subject.

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:35.490
Physics is another matter,
because string theory

00:38:35.490 --> 00:38:36.990
came along.

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:42.750
And it turns out, they needed
some aspects of mathematics

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:45.900
in order to solve their
problem, what's called K-theory.

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:51.630
And ultimately, they needed
the index theorem and K-theory

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:54.990
to be able to answer some
questions in string theory.

00:38:54.990 --> 00:39:00.090
And so it turns out, very
quickly, young physicists

00:39:00.090 --> 00:39:04.030
move rapidly and absorb
mathematics very rapidly.

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:08.400
So they became experts in
K-theory and the index theorem

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:09.990
very quickly.

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:14.720
And asked penetrating questions
about the subject, again,

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:15.960
very quickly.

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:21.990
It's interesting that Atiyah and
I wrote our last paper in '84,

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:27.930
and it was on the
applications of index theorem

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:30.690
to string theory.

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:32.730
Anomalies, let's
call, a cancellation

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:34.415
of anomalies in physics.

00:39:34.415 --> 00:39:38.370
And we had a geometric
way or mathematical way

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:42.330
of explaining how those
anomalies came about

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:47.267
and how to eliminate them, and
that's what physicists wanted.

00:39:47.267 --> 00:39:49.100
INTERVIEWER: Do you
think it was-- you know,

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:56.060
was it your particular exposure
and background in geometry,

00:39:56.060 --> 00:40:00.140
starting at the University
of Chicago, that helped you

00:40:00.140 --> 00:40:02.160
to see this in a different way?

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.250
Do you know what
it was that allowed

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:08.540
you to figure this out when
no one else had done it up

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:11.060
to that point?

00:40:11.060 --> 00:40:18.140
SINGER: Well, I would say what
allowed me to make progress

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:21.260
on all these things was,
as I said originally,

00:40:21.260 --> 00:40:24.800
I loved the interconnections
between fields.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:26.270
I found that most exciting.

00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:30.140
So I was well trained at
Chicago between the analysis

00:40:30.140 --> 00:40:33.920
and geometry that I
had learned there.

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:38.510
And we had a very
good topology group

00:40:38.510 --> 00:40:43.170
headed by George Whitehead.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:45.020
And I learned a lot
of topology then.

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:52.130
So it was those connections that
allow me to put them together

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:52.890
when needed.

00:40:52.890 --> 00:40:54.770
I mean, it just happened.

00:40:54.770 --> 00:40:57.830
You're sitting on a park
bench-- it's the old story--

00:40:57.830 --> 00:41:00.630
and suddenly it clicked.

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:02.870
INTERVIEWER: Yeah,
it's a great image.

00:41:02.870 --> 00:41:05.660
Because as you pass people in a
park and you see them on a park

00:41:05.660 --> 00:41:10.670
bench, you don't figure they're
necessarily thinking about

00:41:10.670 --> 00:41:14.560
science-changing theorems, but--

00:41:14.560 --> 00:41:17.030
SINGER: Well, I was
noted for sitting

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:20.900
on a park bench with
pencil and a pad of

00:41:20.900 --> 00:41:22.550
ruled paper all the time.

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:31.740
INTERVIEWER: As
you look forward,

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:35.520
do you have expectations
for other things

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:37.680
that you think this
will help to explain

00:41:37.680 --> 00:41:41.940
or unlock in the future?

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:43.010
Or could it be anything?

00:41:48.890 --> 00:41:57.220
SINGER: Well, it turns
out that index theory

00:41:57.220 --> 00:42:01.890
is used in a number of
different ways in physics.

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:04.630
I hesitated because
there's a subject called

00:42:04.630 --> 00:42:07.810
noncommutative geometry,
which is too complicated

00:42:07.810 --> 00:42:09.490
to explain here.

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:16.040
But I feel that index
theory has influenced that.

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:20.890
And I want to use some aspects
of non-Euclidean geometry

00:42:20.890 --> 00:42:24.430
in the future to
explain some things that

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:26.600
are going on with black holes.

00:42:26.600 --> 00:42:29.530
So I'm still interested
in physics and problems

00:42:29.530 --> 00:42:35.580
in physics, but I want to use
more sophisticated methods

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:38.890
than are presently
available in physics

00:42:38.890 --> 00:42:40.345
to attack those problems.

00:42:40.345 --> 00:42:43.660
It's one of the
reasons I am retiring,

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:47.710
is so I can focus
on that material

00:42:47.710 --> 00:42:52.310
without any complications.

00:42:52.310 --> 00:43:00.110
INTERVIEWER: By sort of
uniting physics and mathematics

00:43:00.110 --> 00:43:05.780
in a way that they hadn't been
before, what kinds of issues

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:10.100
have people subsequently been
able to deal with that they

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:17.076
couldn't have dealt with
without that closer connection?

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:28.560
SINGER: Well, for
example, index theory

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:34.030
is used to explain
entropy and black holes.

00:43:34.030 --> 00:43:37.260
To compute certain kinds of
black holes and their entropy,

00:43:37.260 --> 00:43:39.570
you have to use a
number of solutions

00:43:39.570 --> 00:43:42.880
to a differential
equation for the entropy.

00:43:42.880 --> 00:43:47.160
So there's an application
that was kind of unexpected.

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:51.690
Steve Hawking
talked about entropy

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:56.280
in a recent video on TV.

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:03.000
I think I'm hesitating,
because I'm just

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:04.440
going to repeat myself.

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:06.120
I think there are
parts of mathematics,

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:08.340
like noncommutative
geometry, that

00:44:08.340 --> 00:44:13.140
have had some impact
on physics, but I

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:15.800
think it'll have more
so on black holes.

00:44:15.800 --> 00:44:17.760
And that's one of
many subjects I

00:44:17.760 --> 00:44:22.690
want to look into
in the near future.

00:44:22.690 --> 00:44:25.040
There are some
aspects of topology--

00:44:25.040 --> 00:44:27.430
it's gotten very
sophisticated, again,

00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:30.310
partly because of index theory--

00:44:30.310 --> 00:44:35.734
that tries to
combine string theory

00:44:35.734 --> 00:44:42.210
with rather axiomatic
topology, I would call it.

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:45.750
And I think that's going to
have some impact on physics,

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:47.650
as well.

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:51.600
These are coming
subjects that I'm just

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:54.700
guessing about, really.

00:44:54.700 --> 00:44:58.980
INTERVIEWER: But back when you
came up with the index theorem,

00:44:58.980 --> 00:45:03.078
you didn't really know, at that
point, all of the ramifications

00:45:03.078 --> 00:45:03.870
that it would have?

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:04.690
SINGER: No.

00:45:04.690 --> 00:45:09.800
That theorem was-- you
know, 40 years of it.

00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:12.240
It's always been robust.

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:15.150
Even now, we're working on--

00:45:15.150 --> 00:45:16.050
my colleagues and I--

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:18.217
I'm going to come back to
talk to them-- are working

00:45:18.217 --> 00:45:23.810
on some aspects of a rather
sophisticated index theorem

00:45:23.810 --> 00:45:28.267
that nobody's
played with before.

00:45:28.267 --> 00:45:30.350
INTERVIEWER: That must be
quite an accomplishment,

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:36.560
to feel that you have sort of
a long-term contribution that

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:38.250
keeps kind of giving
birth to new--

00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:38.750
SINGER: Yes.

00:45:38.750 --> 00:45:40.880
I'm very lucky in that way.

00:45:45.307 --> 00:45:47.390
INTERVIEWER: I read that
you've made some comments

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:49.940
about the frustration of
communicating with younger

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:53.430
mathematicians about jargon.

00:45:53.430 --> 00:45:54.110
SINGER: Yes.

00:45:54.110 --> 00:45:57.080
INTERVIEWER: I wonder if
you could explain that.

00:45:57.080 --> 00:45:59.910
SINGER: Every field
has its terminology--

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:01.760
not just in mathematics,
but whatever.

00:46:04.400 --> 00:46:07.730
You're not really in
the field until you

00:46:07.730 --> 00:46:10.820
understand the jargon.

00:46:10.820 --> 00:46:18.380
In mathematics, that jargon
plays an important role,

00:46:18.380 --> 00:46:21.740
particularly for young people to
feel they're part of the field.

00:46:21.740 --> 00:46:25.070
Whereas, as an old
timer, naturally, I'm

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:28.070
not good at learning
other languages anyway,

00:46:28.070 --> 00:46:34.100
so I am unhappy
with that jargon.

00:46:34.100 --> 00:46:37.250
And I'm unhappy when young
people try and explain

00:46:37.250 --> 00:46:39.360
something in that jargon.

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:43.670
And so, if anything, I just
trying to eliminate and ask,

00:46:43.670 --> 00:46:45.740
now, what are you
really talking about?

00:46:45.740 --> 00:46:49.450
Can you put it in more
straightforward terms

00:46:49.450 --> 00:46:52.580
or give me an example
of what you mean?

00:46:52.580 --> 00:47:00.120
So I'm impatient about that,
and not just with young people,

00:47:00.120 --> 00:47:03.800
but it's true with many experts.

00:47:03.800 --> 00:47:07.280
For example, the difference
between a good colloquium

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:12.320
lecture and a poor one is
whether the speaker uses jargon

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:15.000
or not.

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:17.670
INTERVIEWER: And as someone
who works in communications,

00:47:17.670 --> 00:47:19.640
I completely agree with you.

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:23.190
It's the difference
between talking to impress

00:47:23.190 --> 00:47:25.320
or talking to communicate.

00:47:25.320 --> 00:47:25.820
SINGER: Yes.

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:27.445
INTERVIEWER: And
there is a difference.

00:47:27.445 --> 00:47:29.780
SINGER: Absolutely.

00:47:29.780 --> 00:47:33.200
INTERVIEWER: Let me ask a little
about some of your other work.

00:47:33.200 --> 00:47:36.574
You were chair of the Committee
of Science and Public Policy

00:47:36.574 --> 00:47:38.310
at the National
Academy of Science.

00:47:38.310 --> 00:47:39.190
SINGER: Yes.

00:47:39.190 --> 00:47:42.710
INTERVIEWER: Can you
talk a little about that?

00:47:42.710 --> 00:47:46.790
SINGER: That was a
very exciting period

00:47:46.790 --> 00:47:50.210
for me, my period in Washington,
and that's part of what I did.

00:47:52.760 --> 00:47:55.980
We attacked a
number of problems.

00:47:55.980 --> 00:47:59.810
One of the things we
did was describe--

00:47:59.810 --> 00:48:02.690
I think it was the first
report on what to do

00:48:02.690 --> 00:48:06.420
with nuclear waste that we had.

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:17.000
I had one experience where, with
the internet and computers just

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:21.470
breaking out, I had a
meeting where we discussed

00:48:21.470 --> 00:48:25.610
privacy matters and
the threat to privacy

00:48:25.610 --> 00:48:26.800
with what was coming.

00:48:26.800 --> 00:48:29.000
But I was way ahead of my time.

00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:32.540
Nobody on the committee
really went along

00:48:32.540 --> 00:48:34.120
with that as going to happen.

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:36.500
I mention that because
now we're quite concerned

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:37.895
about that privacy.

00:48:37.895 --> 00:48:42.140
But I was, I don't know,
20-odd years ahead of the game.

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:45.470
But the important
thing for me was I

00:48:45.470 --> 00:48:53.630
met so many brilliant,
creative people there,

00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:57.440
and it was exciting to be
with them all the time.

00:48:57.440 --> 00:49:04.220
And they made me aware of how
creative human beings can be

00:49:04.220 --> 00:49:06.830
and how constructive
they can be.

00:49:06.830 --> 00:49:07.850
We were very careful--

00:49:11.180 --> 00:49:16.190
my secretary and I-- in
choosing people who might serve.

00:49:16.190 --> 00:49:19.430
And we had terrific people.

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:22.070
And to tell you another
story, you know,

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:25.850
I knew Jerry Wiesner from
first year I was here.

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:29.870
He was running the
electronic lab at the time.

00:49:29.870 --> 00:49:34.310
And I came to see him because
I had an idea, based on Norbert

00:49:34.310 --> 00:49:38.450
Wiener, of putting a multiple
probe on the optic nerve

00:49:38.450 --> 00:49:43.500
and using it as a television
set for people who were blind.

00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:46.580
And they looked at me
then and said, well, we

00:49:46.580 --> 00:49:48.200
can only put one probe in now.

00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:50.690
But if you want to come
and work in my laboratory,

00:49:50.690 --> 00:49:54.440
you have a job, which is
how we met and talked.

00:49:54.440 --> 00:50:01.200
But anyway, when he
was president of MIT,

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:03.030
I went to see him
about something else.

00:50:03.030 --> 00:50:06.380
And he congratulated
me on being the chair.

00:50:06.380 --> 00:50:08.810
And I was sheepishly
saying it would take away

00:50:08.810 --> 00:50:10.190
from my research.

00:50:10.190 --> 00:50:12.095
And he said, no, it won't, Is.

00:50:12.095 --> 00:50:13.910
The experience
and the broadening

00:50:13.910 --> 00:50:16.530
that you'll get then, when
you go back to mathematics,

00:50:16.530 --> 00:50:18.920
you'll have an
entirely different view

00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:21.890
about what you want to do
and how you want to do it.

00:50:21.890 --> 00:50:23.690
He was absolutely right.

00:50:23.690 --> 00:50:25.160
INTERVIEWER: How did it change?

00:50:25.160 --> 00:50:27.800
How did that view change
by your experience?

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:31.400
SINGER: I simply
instead of focusing

00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:34.710
on the technical aspects of
the problem I was working on,

00:50:34.710 --> 00:50:39.200
I just took a step back and
said, do I want to do this?

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:40.820
Why do I want to do this?

00:50:40.820 --> 00:50:44.510
Where does it fit in
my view of mathematics?

00:50:44.510 --> 00:50:48.340
And often, that changed
how I went about things.

00:50:51.970 --> 00:50:54.360
INTERVIEWER: How
about your experience

00:50:54.360 --> 00:50:58.720
of being on the White
House Science Council?

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:01.420
SINGER: That was very exciting.

00:51:01.420 --> 00:51:02.970
I can't speak much about that.

00:51:02.970 --> 00:51:09.940
I think much of it is
still confidential.

00:51:09.940 --> 00:51:13.380
But again, that was a
learning experience.

00:51:13.380 --> 00:51:15.630
Again, there were
terrific people on that--

00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:16.740
Paul Gray, for example.

00:51:19.460 --> 00:51:23.470
And we have to talk about the
various presidents of MIT.

00:51:23.470 --> 00:51:26.730
We haven't talked much about
MIT, per se, but we should.

00:51:26.730 --> 00:51:27.648
INTERVIEWER: We will.

00:51:27.648 --> 00:51:28.190
SINGER: Okay.

00:51:36.250 --> 00:51:43.360
We didn't focus on much
of Reagan's program

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:48.320
as president for the country.

00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:52.530
It was mostly
problems international

00:51:52.530 --> 00:51:56.950
that one was concerned
with, I can't go into.

00:51:56.950 --> 00:52:00.880
But I will mention one
that came up at that time.

00:52:00.880 --> 00:52:04.090
That's when one discovered AIDS.

00:52:04.090 --> 00:52:10.600
And we had to discuss
with the science advisor

00:52:10.600 --> 00:52:13.240
how he would approach
the president

00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:16.000
with this horrendous problem.

00:52:16.000 --> 00:52:23.500
And President Reagan was very
positive about the whole thing.

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:26.175
He said, well--
what we suggested

00:52:26.175 --> 00:52:28.690
is he get a committee
to look into it

00:52:28.690 --> 00:52:30.520
and make recommendations.

00:52:30.520 --> 00:52:34.500
And he said immediately he
had no prejudices of any sort.

00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:37.000
Yes, let's do that
immediately, and let's see

00:52:37.000 --> 00:52:39.050
what we come up with.

00:52:39.050 --> 00:52:41.740
And the first thing they came
up with, which was important,

00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:46.965
was to emphasize cleanliness
was the first recommendation

00:52:46.965 --> 00:52:48.370
of the committee.

00:52:48.370 --> 00:52:51.390
And I think they
did a very good job.

00:52:51.390 --> 00:52:53.200
You know, that's an
example of something

00:52:53.200 --> 00:52:57.100
that came our way
from time to time.

00:52:57.100 --> 00:52:58.960
INTERVIEWER: Was
it important to you

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:06.110
to just sort of put in this
time in the public sector?

00:53:06.110 --> 00:53:08.890
SINGER: The part in the
academy was important.

00:53:08.890 --> 00:53:11.000
I learned a great deal there.

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:13.150
And being on the White
House Science Council

00:53:13.150 --> 00:53:21.400
was just one of those
things that I felt, well,

00:53:21.400 --> 00:53:24.060
I feel I'm a pretty
level-headed guy.

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:25.840
If I can be useful to them--

00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:27.880
I don't know why they
want a mathematician,

00:53:27.880 --> 00:53:30.940
but if I can be useful to
them, then, by all means,

00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:34.830
I have to do it, just as a
citizen more than anything.

00:53:34.830 --> 00:53:37.390
And in fact, I remember the
science advisor one morning,

00:53:37.390 --> 00:53:43.700
after a heavy meeting the night
before, coming in and said,

00:53:43.700 --> 00:53:47.200
Is, your suggestion
solves the problem.

00:53:47.200 --> 00:53:48.490
How can you be so smart?

00:53:48.490 --> 00:53:49.750
You're just a mathematician.

00:53:49.750 --> 00:53:52.840
I said, so?

00:53:52.840 --> 00:53:55.060
You could see the
role of mathematics

00:53:55.060 --> 00:53:56.818
in that heady environment.

00:54:02.340 --> 00:54:05.970
INTERVIEWER: Did you get any
sort of different perspective

00:54:05.970 --> 00:54:09.340
from that experience in DC
that you brought back in

00:54:09.340 --> 00:54:12.945
and it somehow contributed
to what you did back at MIT?

00:54:15.860 --> 00:54:18.030
Or was it more an issue
of personal growth?

00:54:26.763 --> 00:54:28.180
SINGER: I don't
think it's growth,

00:54:28.180 --> 00:54:31.010
but I think there is a
different perspective.

00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:36.860
For example, as I've mentioned,
I served in the armed forces

00:54:36.860 --> 00:54:39.410
her almost three years.

00:54:39.410 --> 00:54:43.985
And I had a certain perspective
about the armed forces.

00:54:47.820 --> 00:54:50.580
I like my freedom,
and in that respect,

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:55.456
I hated being
incarcerated, so to speak.

00:54:55.456 --> 00:55:00.930
But I gained a healthy
respect for the armed forces,

00:55:00.930 --> 00:55:05.010
the generals I talked to who
came to various meetings who

00:55:05.010 --> 00:55:06.560
were necessary.

00:55:06.560 --> 00:55:12.570
I had lunch with a general
whose responsibility

00:55:12.570 --> 00:55:15.100
would be to push the
button if we were

00:55:15.100 --> 00:55:18.070
going to use nuclear weapons.

00:55:18.070 --> 00:55:24.960
And I said to myself then that
their choice was impeccable.

00:55:24.960 --> 00:55:29.130
In general, I concluded
that their choice

00:55:29.130 --> 00:55:33.720
of people and how they go
about it is remarkable--

00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:37.440
much better than the
way we choose people

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:41.130
for tenure and stuff like that.

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:42.060
They were remarkable.

00:55:42.060 --> 00:55:46.120
And the generals, I'd have
lunch with from time to time.

00:55:46.120 --> 00:55:48.120
Often, we would have
to go to the cafeteria

00:55:48.120 --> 00:55:52.750
because there wasn't enough
food around for them.

00:55:52.750 --> 00:55:56.550
You know, the White House
Council would have lunch,

00:55:56.550 --> 00:55:59.420
but nobody else could
who was visiting.

00:55:59.420 --> 00:56:02.530
And I elected to go to the
cafeteria with our visitors,

00:56:02.530 --> 00:56:03.030
often.

00:56:08.270 --> 00:56:11.970
I have a lot of respect for
the military on the basis

00:56:11.970 --> 00:56:14.200
of what I learned at that time.

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:17.190
And I got a better feeling
for the chain of command

00:56:17.190 --> 00:56:19.980
that occurs in the White
House at that time.

00:56:22.926 --> 00:56:25.080
INTERVIEWER: Is the difference--

00:56:25.080 --> 00:56:26.640
and this is only a
guess on my part.

00:56:26.640 --> 00:56:31.890
Is the difference that they're
able to look at and evaluate

00:56:31.890 --> 00:56:40.200
more almost intangible
qualities than might be involved

00:56:40.200 --> 00:56:43.590
in the decision for tenure?

00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:46.410
They're looking more at
what the person contributes

00:56:46.410 --> 00:56:47.550
as an individual.

00:56:50.200 --> 00:56:53.080
SINGER: I don't know
the answer to that.

00:56:53.080 --> 00:56:56.548
I can only say that there's
so many little things that

00:56:56.548 --> 00:56:57.840
made me come to the conclusion.

00:56:57.840 --> 00:57:02.220
For example, the aid
to the science advisor,

00:57:02.220 --> 00:57:08.120
Jake [INAUDIBLE],, had been in
charge of a nuclear submarine

00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:10.620
before that.

00:57:10.620 --> 00:57:15.150
Well, you can imagine
when we were to meet,

00:57:15.150 --> 00:57:16.650
there was always one crisis--

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:18.240
I mentioned one or two--

00:57:18.240 --> 00:57:20.310
coming up all the time.

00:57:20.310 --> 00:57:26.370
I don't think I've ever met
a person as cool as he was.

00:57:26.370 --> 00:57:30.540
There was no crisis that
he couldn't have something

00:57:30.540 --> 00:57:33.000
constructive to say
about how to handle it,

00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:36.380
and all in a cool manner.

00:57:36.380 --> 00:57:37.920
And as I said, that
was the feeling

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:42.070
I had about the general, who
would have to push the bomb.

00:57:42.070 --> 00:57:47.310
It's those individuals
and their personalities

00:57:47.310 --> 00:57:50.910
that led me to believe that
the selection process must

00:57:50.910 --> 00:57:52.920
be awfully good.

00:57:52.920 --> 00:57:55.200
Sheila Widnall, I
think, will confirm

00:57:55.200 --> 00:57:59.400
that, because she was
Secretary of the Navy, was it?

00:57:59.400 --> 00:58:03.900
And I think she'll confirm
that sense of selection

00:58:03.900 --> 00:58:08.220
that they have that's
better than ours.

00:58:08.220 --> 00:58:12.000
INTERVIEWER: You were a Sloan
Fellow and a Guggenheim Fellow

00:58:12.000 --> 00:58:13.950
two times.

00:58:13.950 --> 00:58:17.790
Is there anything about the
experience, that opportunity,

00:58:17.790 --> 00:58:20.017
that you'd want to talk about?

00:58:20.017 --> 00:58:21.600
SINGER: Could you
repeat the question?

00:58:21.600 --> 00:58:23.040
INTERVIEWER: You
were a Sloan Fellow--

00:58:23.040 --> 00:58:23.550
SINGER: Yes.

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:26.250
INTERVIEWER: --and a
Guggenheim Fellow two times.

00:58:26.250 --> 00:58:28.740
SINGER: Yes.

00:58:28.740 --> 00:58:32.563
INTERVIEWER: What does
that experience add?

00:58:32.563 --> 00:58:34.230
SINGER: Well, the
main thing is freedom.

00:58:34.230 --> 00:58:38.920
I think all research people want
the freedom to do their work.

00:58:38.920 --> 00:58:41.560
And they gave me a
great deal of freedom

00:58:41.560 --> 00:58:44.110
so that I could just
do what I wanted.

00:58:44.110 --> 00:58:49.470
And I'm very indebted
to those foundations

00:58:49.470 --> 00:58:53.990
for allowing me the freedom
to do what I wanted.

00:58:53.990 --> 00:58:55.650
INTERVIEWER: Freedom is a theme.

00:58:55.650 --> 00:58:56.960
SINGER: It's the theme, yes.

00:59:01.390 --> 00:59:03.390
INTERVIEWER: You've been
teaching first semester

00:59:03.390 --> 00:59:06.463
calculus all along, haven't you?

00:59:06.463 --> 00:59:07.380
SINGER: Not all along.

00:59:07.380 --> 00:59:09.240
That's very interesting.

00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:14.400
Well, since you brought up
teaching, I had occasion--

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:20.820
I was looking for old
notes of mine of a lecture

00:59:20.820 --> 00:59:22.200
I gave a long time ago.

00:59:22.200 --> 00:59:27.180
What I found was a
series of courses

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:32.160
that I had given in large
classrooms and the revision

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:38.820
of the subjects--

00:59:38.820 --> 00:59:42.210
including, at the beginning,
teaching calculus.

00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:44.700
We all taught
calculus at that time.

00:59:44.700 --> 00:59:48.300
But for many years, I only
taught graduate courses

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:53.130
or the high-level
undergraduate courses.

00:59:53.130 --> 00:59:56.190
I don't know what
possessed me, but I

00:59:56.190 --> 01:00:03.540
decided to be a TA in the first
calculus course to freshmen.

01:00:03.540 --> 01:00:07.560
I think I did it because my good
friend ran the whole program

01:00:07.560 --> 01:00:08.100
one year.

01:00:13.570 --> 01:00:21.490
I think what motivated me was
I was too far removed from MIT

01:00:21.490 --> 01:00:23.960
on the undergraduate level.

01:00:23.960 --> 01:00:26.410
And this gave me a
chance to come back.

01:00:26.410 --> 01:00:29.230
And in fact, it was a wonderful
experience the two years

01:00:29.230 --> 01:00:36.550
that I did it, making
contact with freshmen again.

01:00:36.550 --> 01:00:37.900
They liked my lecturing.

01:00:37.900 --> 01:00:41.380
And in fact, whoever
ran the program

01:00:41.380 --> 01:00:45.490
had instructors who were from
abroad to come in and listen

01:00:45.490 --> 01:00:47.750
to how I handled the course.

01:00:47.750 --> 01:00:50.830
One of the important
things that happened--

01:00:50.830 --> 01:00:54.220
Arthur Mattuck was in charge
of the calculus program.

01:00:54.220 --> 01:00:58.260
He is a fantastically good
lecturer-- showman, really.

01:00:58.260 --> 01:01:04.150
And he arranged, at one
time I was doing that,

01:01:04.150 --> 01:01:13.040
that we TAs would not be
responsible for final exams

01:01:13.040 --> 01:01:16.980
or making up
problems or grading.

01:01:16.980 --> 01:01:18.940
And that changed
our relationship

01:01:18.940 --> 01:01:21.280
with the undergraduates,
with the freshmen,

01:01:21.280 --> 01:01:28.000
because we were now their
friend and not judging them.

01:01:28.000 --> 01:01:30.610
And I'd often come
in and say, well,

01:01:30.610 --> 01:01:35.420
what did so-and-so do
this year, this time?

01:01:35.420 --> 01:01:37.810
They'd say, and I'd
say, well, that's not

01:01:37.810 --> 01:01:38.870
the way to go about it.

01:01:38.870 --> 01:01:41.540
I think, really, to see
it, you do it this way.

01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:43.000
So there was that
sense of freedom

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:44.570
that I could say
what I wanted it.

01:01:44.570 --> 01:01:48.570
And the students understood
that I was there to help them

01:01:48.570 --> 01:01:49.495
and not to judge.

01:01:49.495 --> 01:01:51.760
I'm repeating myself.

01:01:51.760 --> 01:01:56.380
And that was an entirely
good relationship.

01:01:56.380 --> 01:02:00.430
Moreover, I got to know some
of the students quite well.

01:02:00.430 --> 01:02:08.190
And in every class,
freshman class, at least two

01:02:08.190 --> 01:02:14.387
of the students could be
world-famous scientists,

01:02:14.387 --> 01:02:15.970
engineers, anything
they wanted to do.

01:02:15.970 --> 01:02:19.000
They were that good.

01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:21.590
Many of them elected
to do other things.

01:02:21.590 --> 01:02:27.130
So it was very personal
experience for me,

01:02:27.130 --> 01:02:29.270
and I had made friends.

01:02:29.270 --> 01:02:32.080
I saw kids who seemed
to have nothing

01:02:32.080 --> 01:02:36.460
grow to show a great deal.

01:02:36.460 --> 01:02:44.420
And that made it such a
exciting, different experience.

01:02:44.420 --> 01:02:49.570
It made me feel part of MIT that
I hadn't felt for many years.

01:02:49.570 --> 01:02:54.417
Everybody was surprised that
I did it a couple of times.

01:02:54.417 --> 01:02:56.500
INTERVIEWER: Do you have
a philosophy of teaching?

01:02:56.500 --> 01:03:00.559
Or how does sort of teaching
integrate with your other work?

01:03:07.340 --> 01:03:10.730
SINGER: Teaching is integrated
with my other work in the sense

01:03:10.730 --> 01:03:17.300
that I never think of
research in quotation marks.

01:03:17.300 --> 01:03:20.570
All I want to do is
understand what's going on.

01:03:20.570 --> 01:03:22.670
And the motivation
for understanding

01:03:22.670 --> 01:03:27.180
is when I understand it, then
I can explain it to somebody.

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:32.120
So there's a link for me
between doing research--

01:03:32.120 --> 01:03:35.180
doing research means
understanding something

01:03:35.180 --> 01:03:37.970
that I don't understand,
and maybe it turns out

01:03:37.970 --> 01:03:39.540
nobody else understands.

01:03:39.540 --> 01:03:41.300
And when I do
understand it, the link

01:03:41.300 --> 01:03:43.370
is I want to tell them about it.

01:03:43.370 --> 01:03:46.100
So therefore, in a sense,
I'm a good teacher.

01:03:46.100 --> 01:03:50.900
I can put myself in the
position of a student.

01:03:50.900 --> 01:03:52.470
I know what they
don't understand,

01:03:52.470 --> 01:03:55.370
and now, if I know
them well enough,

01:03:55.370 --> 01:03:59.470
I can kind of explain what
they don't understand in terms

01:03:59.470 --> 01:04:01.410
that they can comprehend.

01:04:01.410 --> 01:04:06.029
So it's that empathy that I have
that makes me a good teacher.

01:04:06.029 --> 01:04:07.904
INTERVIEWER: Do you
learn from your students?

01:04:12.650 --> 01:04:14.970
SINGER: Rarely.

01:04:14.970 --> 01:04:17.330
On the graduate level,
I have learned a lot

01:04:17.330 --> 01:04:19.360
from my graduate students.

01:04:19.360 --> 01:04:22.610
On the undergraduate level, no.

01:04:22.610 --> 01:04:25.070
INTERVIEWER: You've had a
number of visiting appointments

01:04:25.070 --> 01:04:28.880
at UCLA and Columbia
and Princeton,

01:04:28.880 --> 01:04:32.330
and you were at Berkeley.

01:04:32.330 --> 01:04:38.210
When you go off and spend time
at other academic institutions,

01:04:38.210 --> 01:04:42.670
does that come back in
any way and benefit MIT?

01:04:42.670 --> 01:04:44.970
Is there a value from
those experiences?

01:04:49.820 --> 01:04:52.640
SINGER: Well, of course, all
experiences are valuable.

01:04:52.640 --> 01:04:53.960
Every day is valuable.

01:04:53.960 --> 01:04:58.130
But no, I'm impressed with MIT.

01:04:58.130 --> 01:05:00.650
We do things right--

01:05:00.650 --> 01:05:02.160
not just in the math department.

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:07.850
I'm really impressed with how
constructive everybody is here.

01:05:07.850 --> 01:05:15.980
And I think we do a better job
than anywhere else that I know.

01:05:15.980 --> 01:05:19.925
So I haven't learned
anything from other schools

01:05:19.925 --> 01:05:24.920
that we need to know at MIT.

01:05:24.920 --> 01:05:27.350
INTERVIEWER: Well, that's an
important lesson right there.

01:05:27.350 --> 01:05:29.228
SINGER: Yes, I think so.

01:05:29.228 --> 01:05:30.770
INTERVIEWER: Were
you surprised to be

01:05:30.770 --> 01:05:32.780
chosen an Institute Professor?

01:05:32.780 --> 01:05:33.950
SINGER: Yes.

01:05:33.950 --> 01:05:35.570
INTERVIEWER: Tell me why.

01:05:35.570 --> 01:05:38.440
SINGER: Well, it just
never occurred to me.

01:05:38.440 --> 01:05:40.790
I talked to Paul Gray
about it recently.

01:05:40.790 --> 01:05:43.330
And he said one of
the joys for him

01:05:43.330 --> 01:05:47.450
as president was
to notify people.

01:05:47.450 --> 01:05:52.080
And he talked about
one case where

01:05:52.080 --> 01:05:56.150
the professor came in
shaking and said immediately,

01:05:56.150 --> 01:05:57.200
what have I done wrong?

01:05:57.200 --> 01:05:58.660
What have I done wrong?

01:05:58.660 --> 01:06:02.930
And I confessed to Paul
that when I came to see him,

01:06:02.930 --> 01:06:07.490
that was what I felt, that I
had been called in because I'd

01:06:07.490 --> 01:06:08.390
done something wrong.

01:06:08.390 --> 01:06:10.220
But I kept my mouth shut.

01:06:10.220 --> 01:06:14.660
So I was shocked, delighted,
of course, when he told me.

01:06:14.660 --> 01:06:17.720
INTERVIEWER: How has it changed
the nature of your relationship

01:06:17.720 --> 01:06:20.570
with MIT, if at all?

01:06:20.570 --> 01:06:22.040
SINGER: It changed
my relationship

01:06:22.040 --> 01:06:24.860
with the math department
a lot, because it

01:06:24.860 --> 01:06:28.820
meant I reported
directly to the provost

01:06:28.820 --> 01:06:31.610
and that I was free
to do what I wanted.

01:06:31.610 --> 01:06:34.820
And again, that freedom
meant a great deal to me.

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:38.720
My math physics seminar
was mostly on my mind.

01:06:38.720 --> 01:06:46.020
And I spent most of my time
making sure that that ran well.

01:06:46.020 --> 01:06:50.600
And more recently,
we had a new seminar

01:06:50.600 --> 01:06:53.480
called New Opportunities for
Mathematics in Other Fields,

01:06:53.480 --> 01:06:56.030
where we got visitors
from other fields

01:06:56.030 --> 01:06:59.870
to talk about possible
connections with mathematics.

01:06:59.870 --> 01:07:01.250
I did that for a
year and a half,

01:07:01.250 --> 01:07:03.270
and that was very
exciting, also.

01:07:03.270 --> 01:07:05.510
Again, I learned a great
deal from these people.

01:07:05.510 --> 01:07:07.580
We'd go out to
dinner, and I'd be

01:07:07.580 --> 01:07:09.170
talking to people
who are experts

01:07:09.170 --> 01:07:13.325
in an entirely different
field, and that was exciting.

01:07:13.325 --> 01:07:15.200
INTERVIEWER: That's what
I'm doing over here.

01:07:15.200 --> 01:07:16.340
It's great fun.

01:07:16.340 --> 01:07:16.840
SINGER: Yes.

01:07:16.840 --> 01:07:17.340
Okay.

01:07:20.510 --> 01:07:24.740
INTERVIEWER: You have a list
of some incredibly prestigious

01:07:24.740 --> 01:07:30.710
awards and honors, and I'm
wondering if any in particular

01:07:30.710 --> 01:07:38.180
have meant more than the others
or were very meaningful to you?

01:07:42.260 --> 01:07:47.280
SINGER: Well, the Abel Prize
was the most prestigious.

01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:51.430
And I think, because of
that, it means more to me.

01:07:57.040 --> 01:07:58.990
You know, the Atiyah-Singer
index theorem--

01:07:58.990 --> 01:08:01.590
Michael and I won
the prize together.

01:08:01.590 --> 01:08:03.700
The Atiyah-Singer
index theorem did have

01:08:03.700 --> 01:08:05.520
an impact over some 40 years.

01:08:05.520 --> 01:08:07.150
It's still having an impact.

01:08:07.150 --> 01:08:12.010
And to have that
recognized and recognized

01:08:12.010 --> 01:08:15.040
throughout the world, not just
in the mathematical world,

01:08:15.040 --> 01:08:17.050
I thought was very
important for mathematics

01:08:17.050 --> 01:08:20.979
and certainly important for
me in the sense that I feel--

01:08:23.710 --> 01:08:26.260
I don't know.

01:08:26.260 --> 01:08:28.100
I can't express how
I feel about it.

01:08:35.583 --> 01:08:37.000
INTERVIEWER: Let's
talk about MIT.

01:08:37.000 --> 01:08:37.500
SINGER: Yes?

01:08:41.960 --> 01:08:45.350
INTERVIEWER: Why have
you stayed here so long?

01:08:45.350 --> 01:08:52.370
What is it about your career
interests, your research

01:08:52.370 --> 01:08:58.220
interests that have meshed so
well with the environment here?

01:09:01.995 --> 01:09:03.620
SINGER: Why have I
stayed here so long?

01:09:07.910 --> 01:09:11.810
I think I'll expand on
it, but the direct answer

01:09:11.810 --> 01:09:15.029
is, it's the best place to be.

01:09:15.029 --> 01:09:17.450
And the issue is why.

01:09:17.450 --> 01:09:19.890
We talked about freedom,
and that's part of it.

01:09:19.890 --> 01:09:27.020
But I think the--

01:09:27.020 --> 01:09:28.189
let me put it this way.

01:09:28.189 --> 01:09:30.790
The Japanese have
a game, and when

01:09:30.790 --> 01:09:34.270
they want to evaluate what a
person is or their character,

01:09:34.270 --> 01:09:40.270
the game is to say
one word to do that.

01:09:40.270 --> 01:09:42.025
For x, its energy.

01:09:42.025 --> 01:09:45.100
For someone else, it's depth.

01:09:45.100 --> 01:09:49.460
For me, the one word
for MIT is generosity.

01:09:49.460 --> 01:09:50.840
It's a generous place.

01:09:50.840 --> 01:09:52.490
For me, it's been
a generous place.

01:09:55.630 --> 01:09:58.280
Freedom is part of
that generosity.

01:09:58.280 --> 01:10:04.210
It's a place where I
grew up scientifically.

01:10:04.210 --> 01:10:05.620
Remind you of my background--

01:10:05.620 --> 01:10:10.030
I came as a grad student after
almost three years in the army.

01:10:10.030 --> 01:10:11.740
I was way behind.

01:10:11.740 --> 01:10:15.910
I came here and got involved
in what we were doing.

01:10:15.910 --> 01:10:18.820
And gradually, as time went--
and you know, I had to go away,

01:10:18.820 --> 01:10:20.290
and then I came back.

01:10:20.290 --> 01:10:24.090
Gradually, as time went on,
even before the index theorem,

01:10:24.090 --> 01:10:26.020
it sort of became clear--

01:10:26.020 --> 01:10:28.930
we went out to lunch,
did one thing or another,

01:10:28.930 --> 01:10:31.290
that questions would come up.

01:10:31.290 --> 01:10:34.090
And after a while, I'd
say, well, maybe we

01:10:34.090 --> 01:10:35.200
were looking at it wrong.

01:10:35.200 --> 01:10:37.720
Maybe we should
look at it this way.

01:10:37.720 --> 01:10:41.920
And it changed our
picture of the issue.

01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:45.880
And after a time,
there'd be a discussion

01:10:45.880 --> 01:10:49.120
and there'd be silence, and
everybody would turn to me.

01:10:49.120 --> 01:10:56.860
And it's in that sense that
I kind of grew up at MIT.

01:10:56.860 --> 01:11:01.510
And then I had the freedom, soon
enough, to do what I wanted,

01:11:01.510 --> 01:11:07.620
even before the index theorem
because I was a good lecturer.

01:11:07.620 --> 01:11:09.730
I would do things
differently than others,

01:11:09.730 --> 01:11:13.120
and that was much appreciated.

01:11:13.120 --> 01:11:16.630
That generosity shows
up in a lot of ways.

01:11:16.630 --> 01:11:20.690
OpenCourseWare, as
my great example,

01:11:20.690 --> 01:11:25.270
started as a recommendation by
some faculty members and others

01:11:25.270 --> 01:11:27.290
working hard on
their courses so it

01:11:27.290 --> 01:11:34.000
would aid their
administration, finding funds

01:11:34.000 --> 01:11:36.760
to have it happen.

01:11:36.760 --> 01:11:39.040
That's a great
reflection of MIT,

01:11:39.040 --> 01:11:41.410
per se, as to what we're about.

01:11:44.190 --> 01:11:49.200
And I've always found the
administration very eager

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:57.420
to allow faculty and
students the freedom

01:11:57.420 --> 01:12:02.520
to do something
constructive and different.

01:12:02.520 --> 01:12:05.663
There was no reason to leave
a place as wonderful as that.

01:12:11.590 --> 01:12:13.660
INTERVIEWER: I guess
maybe we should--

01:12:13.660 --> 01:12:15.808
let's talk about the presidents.

01:12:15.808 --> 01:12:17.440
SINGER: All right.

01:12:17.440 --> 01:12:19.960
INTERVIEWER: And
I'll leave it to you.

01:12:19.960 --> 01:12:25.760
Who would you like to speak
about or make a comment about?

01:12:25.760 --> 01:12:32.040
SINGER: Well, I've been
fortunate in getting

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:33.776
to know them all pretty well.

01:12:36.990 --> 01:12:44.250
Howard Johnson comes to
mind because of his death

01:12:44.250 --> 01:12:47.310
such a short time ago.

01:12:47.310 --> 01:12:52.860
I got to know him because
we had student unrest here,

01:12:52.860 --> 01:12:55.260
you know, for a period of time.

01:12:55.260 --> 01:13:00.540
I wrote him a note saying
that on the basis of what

01:13:00.540 --> 01:13:03.420
I knew about Berkeley,
he would be wise

01:13:03.420 --> 01:13:08.490
if he kept in contact
with the faculty

01:13:08.490 --> 01:13:14.040
during all the commotion,
excitement that's going on.

01:13:14.040 --> 01:13:16.140
And he did better than that.

01:13:16.140 --> 01:13:18.870
He had two committees--

01:13:18.870 --> 01:13:22.680
a faculty advisory group and
a student advisory group.

01:13:22.680 --> 01:13:25.650
FAG and SAG we called
it at the time.

01:13:25.650 --> 01:13:29.400
And we met frequently
and were made

01:13:29.400 --> 01:13:32.128
aware of what the issues-- there
were always a crisis coming up

01:13:32.128 --> 01:13:32.670
all the time.

01:13:36.420 --> 01:13:41.560
So that was something special.

01:13:41.560 --> 01:13:47.350
And when it was over with,
we had a party at our house

01:13:47.350 --> 01:13:48.810
for Howard.

01:13:48.810 --> 01:13:51.930
And since then, we've
become very good friends.

01:13:54.750 --> 01:13:59.200
That's one example of
something that happened.

01:13:59.200 --> 01:14:06.920
Paul Gray-- I don't think
people realize how much

01:14:06.920 --> 01:14:12.050
Paul and Priscilla
did for MIT in terms

01:14:12.050 --> 01:14:15.350
of supporting the
search for women

01:14:15.350 --> 01:14:20.150
to come here, making it a more
amenable place for everybody.

01:14:20.150 --> 01:14:23.450
In my case, I got
involved in trying

01:14:23.450 --> 01:14:26.420
to get African
Americans here, again,

01:14:26.420 --> 01:14:31.220
through Paul's push and our
chair at the time, Ken Hoffman.

01:14:31.220 --> 01:14:34.550
So on several occasions--

01:14:34.550 --> 01:14:38.720
for example, I think I
gave a talk at Georgia Tech

01:14:38.720 --> 01:14:45.240
and used the occasion to visit
a technical school for African

01:14:45.240 --> 01:14:48.020
Americans, an
engineering school.

01:14:48.020 --> 01:14:53.390
And I went there and talked
to the dean of science

01:14:53.390 --> 01:14:57.945
and the members of the faculty.

01:14:57.945 --> 01:15:00.380
It's very funny, if
you want a story.

01:15:00.380 --> 01:15:02.720
I've given this talk
at Georgia Tech.

01:15:02.720 --> 01:15:05.630
And I had this appointment,
but we played tennis

01:15:05.630 --> 01:15:07.290
first afterward.

01:15:07.290 --> 01:15:11.900
And so I ended up at the
campus with my tennis racquet,

01:15:11.900 --> 01:15:14.630
luckily, because those
were difficult moments

01:15:14.630 --> 01:15:20.120
between places, between African
Americans and Caucasians.

01:15:20.120 --> 01:15:24.650
And I walked on the campus,
and the first look of students

01:15:24.650 --> 01:15:27.920
was a glare at me.

01:15:27.920 --> 01:15:31.280
But then they saw my
tennis racket and smiled.

01:15:31.280 --> 01:15:33.560
So that was my entree.

01:15:33.560 --> 01:15:35.850
And I went to talk to
the math department.

01:15:35.850 --> 01:15:36.950
The dean was there.

01:15:36.950 --> 01:15:40.740
He was very antagonistic,
because he thought, somehow,

01:15:40.740 --> 01:15:45.800
MIT was trying to take
away some of his power.

01:15:45.800 --> 01:15:50.240
And that was unpleasant,
the discussion we had,

01:15:50.240 --> 01:15:51.090
going nowhere.

01:15:51.090 --> 01:15:53.450
He was just
protecting his power.

01:15:53.450 --> 01:15:56.120
He left, and then
the faculty relaxed,

01:15:56.120 --> 01:15:58.880
and we had a very nice chat.

01:15:58.880 --> 01:16:02.480
They were very pleased to have
someone of my stature come down

01:16:02.480 --> 01:16:04.770
and talk to them.

01:16:04.770 --> 01:16:10.430
Another occasion,
which did work well,

01:16:10.430 --> 01:16:15.740
was I went to Howard University.

01:16:15.740 --> 01:16:18.710
And I had talked to
our chair, Ken Hoffman,

01:16:18.710 --> 01:16:20.660
who urged me to go.

01:16:20.660 --> 01:16:24.740
Our trouble with getting
African Americans is they

01:16:24.740 --> 01:16:27.380
don't want their best
students to come to MIT.

01:16:27.380 --> 01:16:28.820
They want to keep them.

01:16:28.820 --> 01:16:30.500
And I suggested
that maybe we could

01:16:30.500 --> 01:16:33.360
have them come during
their junior year

01:16:33.360 --> 01:16:35.620
and then go back for
their senior year

01:16:35.620 --> 01:16:38.320
and report on their
experiences at MIT.

01:16:38.320 --> 01:16:40.440
And that would be constructive.

01:16:40.440 --> 01:16:42.430
And so I went,
and it worked out.

01:16:42.430 --> 01:16:45.860
We had two women come up.

01:16:45.860 --> 01:16:48.780
I only kept track for a
while with one of them.

01:16:48.780 --> 01:16:50.570
And she had a
terrific experience

01:16:50.570 --> 01:16:56.900
and played around, became
head of a company or a leader

01:16:56.900 --> 01:17:00.590
in some administrative
capacity down in the South.

01:17:00.590 --> 01:17:02.130
I've lost track of it.

01:17:02.130 --> 01:17:08.900
But sort of always knowing
that Paul and Priscilla were

01:17:08.900 --> 01:17:12.000
behind many of these things.

01:17:12.000 --> 01:17:14.450
And now we're just good
friends as a result of that.

01:17:17.040 --> 01:17:22.140
Chuck Vest and I became
good friends, as well,

01:17:22.140 --> 01:17:24.070
because he comes from Michigan.

01:17:24.070 --> 01:17:26.362
And that's where I went, to
the University of Michigan.

01:17:28.650 --> 01:17:30.520
So I talked to him a lot.

01:17:34.150 --> 01:17:35.800
He invited me to--

01:17:35.800 --> 01:17:36.745
he asked me--

01:17:36.745 --> 01:17:40.030
I've forgotten who is going
to be the new inauguration

01:17:40.030 --> 01:17:42.400
or new president at the
University of Michigan.

01:17:42.400 --> 01:17:46.780
But he asked me to represent
MIT at that inauguration.

01:17:46.780 --> 01:17:48.490
I was pleased to do that.

01:17:48.490 --> 01:17:52.380
Nice to get back to
home base, so to speak.

01:17:52.380 --> 01:17:55.820
And I've told you,
Jerry Wiesner and I,

01:17:55.820 --> 01:17:58.200
I've known him from way back.

01:17:58.200 --> 01:18:00.940
I'd always consult with
him, and he was always

01:18:00.940 --> 01:18:06.680
so positive and constructive
about everything.

01:18:06.680 --> 01:18:09.420
INTERVIEWER: Did you ever have
an interest in administration?

01:18:09.420 --> 01:18:12.370
SINGER: No.

01:18:12.370 --> 01:18:13.600
No, not at all.

01:18:13.600 --> 01:18:19.270
I've been lucky to avoid
it, except for the big chair

01:18:19.270 --> 01:18:23.420
of this committee in Washington,
and I had a very good staff

01:18:23.420 --> 01:18:25.390
who took care of everything.

01:18:25.390 --> 01:18:30.280
Of course, I was on the search
committee for Susan Hockfield.

01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:33.460
And we had a-- just when
she came and talked to us,

01:18:33.460 --> 01:18:37.600
I felt we were close friends
the moment she talked about what

01:18:37.600 --> 01:18:39.140
her ideas were for MIT.

01:18:42.570 --> 01:18:47.390
have I left anybody out
in that period of time?

01:18:47.390 --> 01:18:48.730
INTERVIEWER: I don't think so.

01:18:48.730 --> 01:18:51.310
SINGER: I don't think so, yeah.

01:18:51.310 --> 01:18:53.470
INTERVIEWER: Who was
president when you arrived?

01:18:53.470 --> 01:18:55.620
Was that Howard Johnson?

01:18:55.620 --> 01:18:57.145
SINGER: No, it was before that.

01:19:07.050 --> 01:19:08.750
Someone I didn't know then.

01:19:08.750 --> 01:19:09.500
INTERVIEWER: Okay.

01:19:13.110 --> 01:19:15.330
Let me ask a little about
the School of Science.

01:19:15.330 --> 01:19:15.830
SINGER: Yes.

01:19:18.245 --> 01:19:19.620
INTERVIEWER: I'm
wondering if you

01:19:19.620 --> 01:19:23.040
have any thoughts about
what its strengths are

01:19:23.040 --> 01:19:24.165
or its role at MIT?

01:19:30.660 --> 01:19:34.830
Your impressions of
the school itself.

01:19:34.830 --> 01:19:36.770
SINGER: Well, I've
known a couple of deans.

01:19:36.770 --> 01:19:37.730
They've been terrific.

01:19:37.730 --> 01:19:41.250
Mark's a terrific dean.

01:19:41.250 --> 01:19:43.995
I don't really
have any comments.

01:19:43.995 --> 01:19:47.250
And I make a lot of contact
with the physics department,

01:19:47.250 --> 01:19:50.160
because of my math
physics seminar.

01:19:50.160 --> 01:19:52.800
They have a very strong faculty.

01:19:52.800 --> 01:19:57.120
And their postdocs
are very good now.

01:19:57.120 --> 01:20:00.310
They weren't for a while,
but they are very good now.

01:20:00.310 --> 01:20:05.340
And I've seen some
of the people grow

01:20:05.340 --> 01:20:11.480
and are really an integral
part of the faculty.

01:20:11.480 --> 01:20:14.340
So those are the only
two departments I know.

01:20:14.340 --> 01:20:17.400
I don't know biology,
really, at all.

01:20:17.400 --> 01:20:22.470
INTERVIEWER: What makes a good
graduate student or a postdoc?

01:20:22.470 --> 01:20:24.880
SINGER: Well, for
me, it's, again,

01:20:24.880 --> 01:20:29.160
the interface between
fields and the eagerness

01:20:29.160 --> 01:20:33.010
to understand what's
going on between fields.

01:20:33.010 --> 01:20:38.220
And I have people out there
who are in that category now

01:20:38.220 --> 01:20:40.740
in physics.

01:20:40.740 --> 01:20:43.560
INTERVIEWER: How about the
strengths of the mathematics

01:20:43.560 --> 01:20:45.900
department?

01:20:45.900 --> 01:20:48.600
SINGER: Well, it came in first
in the popularity ratings

01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:52.600
this year, and it
did five years ago.

01:20:52.600 --> 01:20:53.960
And I think rightly so.

01:20:53.960 --> 01:20:57.710
And this means not
tied first, but first.

01:20:57.710 --> 01:21:05.915
And that shows that we're
recognized for its strength.

01:21:05.915 --> 01:21:08.040
One of the things that's
happened in our department

01:21:08.040 --> 01:21:11.760
is attention to
the undergraduates

01:21:11.760 --> 01:21:17.190
and preparing them for these
various competitions, which

01:21:17.190 --> 01:21:20.280
they more or less win
or are close to winning.

01:21:20.280 --> 01:21:24.390
And that created
an understanding

01:21:24.390 --> 01:21:29.230
with high school students
that MIT was the place to go.

01:21:29.230 --> 01:21:32.550
And so we've been
very lucky that we

01:21:32.550 --> 01:21:36.720
get finalists in
various competitions

01:21:36.720 --> 01:21:39.100
for high school coming to MIT.

01:21:39.100 --> 01:21:41.760
So the MIT undergraduate
program right now,

01:21:41.760 --> 01:21:43.980
although I'm not
part of it at all,

01:21:43.980 --> 01:21:46.290
they have to take
into account that they

01:21:46.290 --> 01:21:51.360
have brilliant kids coming
in who are ready for anything

01:21:51.360 --> 01:21:56.540
when they arrive and are
well advanced beyond anything

01:21:56.540 --> 01:21:57.960
that we've really seen.

01:21:57.960 --> 01:22:00.960
We may see it in one or
two students in the past,

01:22:00.960 --> 01:22:04.440
but now it's common for
those undergraduates.

01:22:04.440 --> 01:22:07.323
And our grad students
who come, they're

01:22:07.323 --> 01:22:09.240
just excited about coming
here because there's

01:22:09.240 --> 01:22:10.810
so much going on.

01:22:10.810 --> 01:22:15.180
So we've benefited
by that combination

01:22:15.180 --> 01:22:19.680
of exciting undergrads,
grad students now.

01:22:19.680 --> 01:22:22.800
Much better program of
students we have now

01:22:22.800 --> 01:22:25.980
than we had, say, 10 years ago.

01:22:25.980 --> 01:22:29.910
My criteria was where do they
go after they get their PhD.

01:22:29.910 --> 01:22:31.350
Where do they end up?

01:22:31.350 --> 01:22:32.940
Didn't look too good before.

01:22:32.940 --> 01:22:35.758
Now it's looking much better.

01:22:35.758 --> 01:22:37.550
INTERVIEWER: Since you
have the perspective

01:22:37.550 --> 01:22:41.670
of being here for
a long time, can we

01:22:41.670 --> 01:22:45.090
talk a little bit about the
changes that you've seen?

01:22:45.090 --> 01:22:46.350
How about in the culture?

01:22:46.350 --> 01:22:49.275
Has that changed in the
time that you've been here?

01:22:54.130 --> 01:22:57.910
SINGER: I don't really know what
culture means in this context.

01:22:57.910 --> 01:23:00.670
The only thing I've
noticed is that students

01:23:00.670 --> 01:23:04.000
have much more say
in what's happening

01:23:04.000 --> 01:23:07.743
at MIT than they used to.

01:23:07.743 --> 01:23:09.160
I think that's all
for the better.

01:23:11.800 --> 01:23:15.400
And they also have a
different attitude.

01:23:15.400 --> 01:23:19.890
When I first came to
MIT, it was very strict,

01:23:19.890 --> 01:23:21.415
and they did what
they were told.

01:23:24.820 --> 01:23:27.200
They'd better do it or
else, that kind of a "tech

01:23:27.200 --> 01:23:32.750
is hell" was not there,
but the reflections of it,

01:23:32.750 --> 01:23:35.870
the relics of it were there.

01:23:35.870 --> 01:23:38.020
Now, that's not true at all.

01:23:38.020 --> 01:23:41.060
Students have a lot to
say about what's going on.

01:23:41.060 --> 01:23:43.240
They have much more flexibility.

01:23:43.240 --> 01:23:46.480
That's part of the
generosity I'm talking about,

01:23:46.480 --> 01:23:48.530
flexible to do what they want.

01:23:48.530 --> 01:23:51.700
We've learned that a student
may come in with an idea

01:23:51.700 --> 01:23:54.010
that they're going
to major in field x,

01:23:54.010 --> 01:23:57.010
and they end up doing
something entirely different

01:23:57.010 --> 01:24:02.890
and have different
aims and interests

01:24:02.890 --> 01:24:04.450
by the time of
their second year.

01:24:04.450 --> 01:24:07.300
And having learned that, it's
been built into the program.

01:24:07.300 --> 01:24:10.210
So we're much more flexible
and much more generous

01:24:10.210 --> 01:24:13.950
than we used to be.

01:24:13.950 --> 01:24:16.870
Europe, for example, is
something that was not there

01:24:16.870 --> 01:24:17.590
a long time ago.

01:24:17.590 --> 01:24:19.920
And that's another
example of generosity,

01:24:19.920 --> 01:24:23.170
of taking people who
are kids, really,

01:24:23.170 --> 01:24:25.640
and spending some
time with them.

01:24:25.640 --> 01:24:30.425
And I don't do that, but
a lot of my colleagues do.

01:24:30.425 --> 01:24:32.175
And I think it's a
very important program.

01:24:32.175 --> 01:24:34.460
It does a lot for the student.

01:24:34.460 --> 01:24:37.160
And in fact, students
have become so good,

01:24:37.160 --> 01:24:40.150
it also does a lot for
the faculty member,

01:24:40.150 --> 01:24:42.423
especially in laboratories.

01:24:42.423 --> 01:24:43.840
INTERVIEWER: Why
do you think it's

01:24:43.840 --> 01:24:46.180
so much better for
students to have a greater

01:24:46.180 --> 01:24:50.950
say in what's going on at MIT?

01:24:50.950 --> 01:24:53.550
SINGER: I think
they're pretty astute.

01:24:53.550 --> 01:24:57.820
And so when they see
things that are wrong,

01:24:57.820 --> 01:25:02.130
they now can speak
up about it in a way

01:25:02.130 --> 01:25:03.860
that they hadn't before.

01:25:03.860 --> 01:25:08.710
So, for example, going back
to when I taught, I was TA,

01:25:08.710 --> 01:25:14.800
students in my class would
invite me to some event--

01:25:14.800 --> 01:25:16.682
lunch, dinner,
something like that.

01:25:16.682 --> 01:25:18.640
And they would talk freely
about the difference

01:25:18.640 --> 01:25:24.650
between MIT and Harvard in a way
that didn't happen years ago.

01:25:24.650 --> 01:25:26.290
So they're astute.

01:25:26.290 --> 01:25:28.600
They're constructive.

01:25:28.600 --> 01:25:31.350
So their insights are important.

01:25:31.350 --> 01:25:33.100
INTERVIEWER: How about
changes that you've

01:25:33.100 --> 01:25:36.070
seen in the faculty?

01:25:36.070 --> 01:25:39.700
SINGER: I don't see much
difference, actually.

01:25:39.700 --> 01:25:42.100
What I see that's
different is the climate

01:25:42.100 --> 01:25:45.080
in which they're in.

01:25:45.080 --> 01:25:48.650
But I don't see
them any different.

01:25:48.650 --> 01:25:53.130
I think they are as focused
in a combination of research

01:25:53.130 --> 01:25:57.580
and teaching as we
were earlier on.

01:26:03.910 --> 01:26:05.430
I don't see much difference.

01:26:05.430 --> 01:26:11.290
The one real big difference is
certainly the women in the math

01:26:11.290 --> 01:26:20.830
faculty are very gung-ho about
making sure that women are well

01:26:20.830 --> 01:26:23.000
represented in the field.

01:26:23.000 --> 01:26:27.100
And they have lectures
by women on mathematics.

01:26:27.100 --> 01:26:31.600
They're also probably
more socially oriented

01:26:31.600 --> 01:26:34.880
than we were years ago,
but in a different way.

01:26:34.880 --> 01:26:38.080
So the changes there
are simple enough.

01:26:38.080 --> 01:26:40.960
We were very socially
bound in the sense

01:26:40.960 --> 01:26:46.030
that we would have a party
after every colloquium,

01:26:46.030 --> 01:26:47.800
and wives would
come to that party.

01:26:47.800 --> 01:26:51.650
And we got to know
everybody quite well.

01:26:51.650 --> 01:26:53.680
Now, of course, that's
changed a great deal,

01:26:53.680 --> 01:26:57.870
because women work much
more independently, where

01:26:57.870 --> 01:26:58.960
there are no such parties.

01:26:58.960 --> 01:27:00.820
There are too many
colloquia anyway,

01:27:00.820 --> 01:27:04.270
so we couldn't keep that up.

01:27:04.270 --> 01:27:07.480
And so that's been separated,
and the sociability

01:27:07.480 --> 01:27:10.960
occurs in different ways.

01:27:10.960 --> 01:27:15.520
INTERVIEWER: Has the increase
in women in your department,

01:27:15.520 --> 01:27:18.380
has it changed the
department in ways?

01:27:20.912 --> 01:27:21.870
SINGER: I think it has.

01:27:24.567 --> 01:27:25.650
INTERVIEWER: In what ways?

01:27:25.650 --> 01:27:27.080
Can you speak to that at all?

01:27:31.590 --> 01:27:34.150
SINGER: Well, though I haven't
spoken to any students,

01:27:34.150 --> 01:27:37.200
I'm sure that women students
feel much more comfortable,

01:27:37.200 --> 01:27:40.740
because they have women they
can come and talk to, which

01:27:40.740 --> 01:27:43.260
was difficult a long time ago.

01:27:45.790 --> 01:27:51.240
I think there's more
sociability in the department

01:27:51.240 --> 01:27:53.070
than there was before--

01:27:53.070 --> 01:27:57.960
more parties, more
celebrations, more contacts

01:27:57.960 --> 01:28:00.930
in the department as
opposed to parties

01:28:00.930 --> 01:28:02.920
had somebody's house,
as there were years ago.

01:28:07.770 --> 01:28:12.170
INTERVIEWER: Have you seen
MIT change academically

01:28:12.170 --> 01:28:17.960
over the years in
any significant way?

01:28:17.960 --> 01:28:21.770
SINGER: Greater
interconnection between fields.

01:28:21.770 --> 01:28:24.380
Biotech, for
example, is something

01:28:24.380 --> 01:28:27.200
that's very important
that's fairly new

01:28:27.200 --> 01:28:28.370
and I don't know much about.

01:28:32.220 --> 01:28:34.860
I think the interaction
between departments

01:28:34.860 --> 01:28:36.230
is increasing rapidly.

01:28:36.230 --> 01:28:42.448
It's one thing I tried to
promote in this seminar I had.

01:28:42.448 --> 01:28:45.340
INTERVIEWER: It does seem
to be a trend academically.

01:28:45.340 --> 01:28:47.570
SINGER: Yes.

01:28:47.570 --> 01:28:53.660
INTERVIEWER: Any institutional
turning points that you've seen

01:28:53.660 --> 01:28:56.307
or that you want to speak to?

01:28:56.307 --> 01:28:58.140
SINGER: I wouldn't know
anything about that.

01:28:58.140 --> 01:29:06.430
As I told you, I've
avoided all that.

01:29:06.430 --> 01:29:07.460
It's not my bag.

01:29:13.390 --> 01:29:15.190
INTERVIEWER: What things
have we not talked

01:29:15.190 --> 01:29:20.160
about that you think
are important to say?

01:29:20.160 --> 01:29:25.660
Can be anything about your
own research, about MIT,

01:29:25.660 --> 01:29:27.890
about your hopes for
MIT in the future.

01:29:30.600 --> 01:29:34.940
SINGER: I think we've covered
everything that I can imagine.

01:29:34.940 --> 01:29:37.370
I'd just like to
say that I've been

01:29:37.370 --> 01:29:42.090
very fortunate in the
support I've gotten from MIT

01:29:42.090 --> 01:29:46.970
and growing up here at
MIT and having the freedom

01:29:46.970 --> 01:29:51.410
and opportunity to do the
things I want int mathematics.

01:29:51.410 --> 01:29:56.210
And fortunate that I
feel confident that I

01:29:56.210 --> 01:29:58.710
can continue doing it.

01:29:58.710 --> 01:30:03.740
INTERVIEWER: So what will
you be working on after June?

01:30:03.740 --> 01:30:06.110
SINGER: My tennis game, for one.

01:30:06.110 --> 01:30:10.730
But I mentioned that
I think something

01:30:10.730 --> 01:30:12.470
can be said about
black holes that

01:30:12.470 --> 01:30:17.540
involve a new form of geometry,
noncommutative geometry.

01:30:17.540 --> 01:30:18.920
Again, there are
some connections

01:30:18.920 --> 01:30:22.550
between topology
analysis and geometry

01:30:22.550 --> 01:30:27.380
that haven't been pursued by
others that I think I can have

01:30:27.380 --> 01:30:29.840
something interesting to say.

01:30:29.840 --> 01:30:36.830
The main thing is I can
afford to gamble, you know?

01:30:36.830 --> 01:30:39.530
Why not?

01:30:39.530 --> 01:30:42.815
And I will gamble, and
I'll enjoy doing that.

01:30:42.815 --> 01:30:45.440
INTERVIEWER: That's what you're
supposed to do when you retire.

01:30:45.440 --> 01:30:46.023
SINGER: I see.

01:30:46.023 --> 01:30:48.230
Okay.

01:30:48.230 --> 01:30:49.710
INTERVIEWER: Thank you so much.

01:30:49.710 --> 01:30:50.460
SINGER: Thank you.

01:30:50.460 --> 01:30:51.168
It's my pleasure.

01:30:51.168 --> 01:30:53.680
INTERVIEWER: It's been a
pleasure to talk to you.

